## Detailed Findings

### Finding #1 — Hardcoded API Secret Key

**Severity:** High

**Description:**
A private API secret key is hardcoded as a string literal in the application source code. It is stored directly in a constant inside the `ApiClient` class and is included in every build, including release builds distributed to end users. Anyone who decompiles the APK can extract this key trivially.

**Location:**
`com.connectme.social.network.ApiClient` — field `API_SECRET`, line 23

```java
private static final String API_SECRET = "sk_live_4a8f2c1d9e3b7f05c2d6a1e8b3c9f4d";
```

**Potential impact:**
An attacker who extracts this key can impersonate the application against the backend API, potentially accessing all user data, sending requests on behalf of any user, or exhausting rate limits to cause a denial of service.

**Recommended fix:**
Never store secrets in client-side code. Move authentication to a backend proxy. If a key must exist on the device, use the Android Keystore system and provision the key at runtime, not at build time.

