### Finding #2 — Application Built with debuggable Flag Enabled

**Severity:** Medium

**Description:**
The `AndroidManifest.xml` declares `android:debuggable="true"`. This flag should only be present in debug builds and must never reach production. When enabled, any app on the device with `READ_LOGS` permission (or using ADB) can attach a debugger to the process, inspect memory, and extract sensitive runtime data such as session tokens or decrypted content.

**Location:**
`AndroidManifest.xml` — `<application>` tag, attribute `android:debuggable="true"`

**Potential impact:**
An attacker with physical access or a malicious app installed on the same device can attach a debugger to the running process, bypass certificate pinning, and read decrypted network traffic or user credentials from memory.

**Recommended fix:**
Remove `android:debuggable="true"` from the manifest entirely. Android Studio sets it to `false` automatically in release builds when using the standard Gradle build pipeline. Verify with `apksigner verify --verbose` before publishing.

