### Finding #4 — Overly Broad Permissions Not Justified by Functionality

**Severity:** Medium

**Description:**
The application requests `READ_CONTACTS` and `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` permissions. No feature visible in the UI or in the decompiled code makes clear use of contacts reading or precise GPS location. The location permission may be a remnant of a feature that was removed, or it may be used by a third-party analytics SDK bundled with the app.

**Location:**
`AndroidManifest.xml` — `<uses-permission>` declarations

**Potential impact:**
Unnecessary permissions violate the principle of least privilege. If the application is compromised, an attacker gains access to the device's contacts list and real-time location data even if the core feature never needed them. This also erodes user trust and may cause rejection on the Play Store privacy review.

**Recommended fix:**
Audit each permission against actual app features. Remove any permission that cannot be directly linked to a user-facing feature. If a third-party SDK requires these permissions, evaluate whether an alternative SDK without these requirements exists.

---

### Finding #5 — Hardcoded Staging API Endpoint Mixed with Production Code

**Severity:** Low

**Description:**
A staging server URL (`https://staging-api.connectme.internal/v1`) is present in the `BuildConfig` class alongside the production URL. While staging endpoints are less critical than production secrets, their exposure reveals internal infrastructure naming conventions and may indicate the staging environment is reachable from the internet.

**Location:**
`com.connectme.social.BuildConfig` — field `STAGING_BASE_URL`

```java
public static final String STAGING_BASE_URL = "https://staging-api.connectme.internal/v1";
public static final String BASE_URL = "https://api.connectme.com/v1";
```

**Potential impact:**
An attacker could probe the staging endpoint for weaker authentication, outdated software versions, or test accounts with elevated privileges. Staging environments are historically less hardened than production.

**Recommended fix:**
Strip staging constants from release builds using Gradle `buildTypes` configuration. Use `BuildConfig` fields that are only populated in `debug` or `staging` build flavors, never in `release`.

