## JADX vs JD-GUI Comparison

Both tools were used to decompile `classes.dex` extracted from the APK. The class `com.connectme.social.network.ApiClient` was used as the reference for comparison.

| Aspect | JADX GUI | JD-GUI |
|---|---|---|
| **Navigation** | Full Android structure — manifest, resources, and code in one panel | Java packages only — no manifest or resource access |
| **Kotlin lambdas** | Readable — correctly reconstructs lambda bodies | Often emits unreadable anonymous class wrappers |
| **Obfuscated names** | Attempts heuristic renaming (`a` → inferred from usage) | Keeps obfuscated names as-is (`a`, `b`, `c`) |
| **Resource access** | Inline access to `strings.xml`, `network_security_config.xml` | No resource access — requires manual extraction |
| **Finding #1 discovery** | Found directly in source tree under `network/ApiClient` | Required knowing the class name in advance |

**Conclusion:** JADX GUI is significantly more practical for Android-specific analysis. JD-GUI remains useful as a quick second opinion on specific class decompilation output when JADX produces ambiguous results.

